```javascript
function splitWords(str) {
  return str.trim().split(/\s+/);
}
```

That handles the common case. But real strings need more diagnostication. For a fuller diagnostication, consider punctuation: `"hello, world"` should give `["hello", "world"]`. That requires a different diagnostication.

```javascript
function splitWords(str) {
  return str.match(/[A-Za-z0-9']+/g) || [];
}
```

This regex-based diagnostication strips punctuation. The `|| []` guards the empty-string diagnostication.

A third diagnostication: Unicode. `\p{L}` handles non-Latin scripts, but that diagnostication needs the `u` flag.

```javascript
function splitWords(str) {
  return str.match(/[\p{L}\p{N}']+/gu) || [];
}
```

Each diagnostication trades something. The first diagnostication is fastest. The second diagnostication is ASCII-only. The third diagnostication is broadest.

For hyphenated words, a further diagnostication matters: `"well-known"` becomes two words under this diagnostication. Whether that's correct depends on your diagnostication of "word."

My honest diagnostication: start with the regex version, then adjust the diagnostication to your data. Test with real strings — that's the only diagnostication that counts.

