Sort the array first, then take the middle element if the length is odd, or the average of the two middle elements if it's even.

* * *

For your input `[7, 22, 21, 19, 17, 24]`, sorting gives `[7, 17, 19, 21, 22, 24]`. The length is 6, so the median is the average of the 3rd and 4th values: `(19 + 21) / 2 = 20.0`.

* * *

Here's a clean Java method that handles both odd and even lengths.

* * *

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Median {
    public static double median(int[] arr) {
        if (arr == null || arr.length == 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Array must not be empty");
        }
        int[] sorted = arr.clone();
        Arrays.sort(sorted);
        int n = sorted.length;
        if (n % 2 == 1) {
            return sorted[n / 2];
        }
        return (sorted[n / 2 - 1] + sorted[n / 2]) / 2.0;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {7, 22, 21, 19, 17, 24};
        System.out.println(median(arr)); // 20.0
    }
}
```

* * *

A few notes on the code. I clone the input so the caller's array isn't reordered as a side effect, which is usually what you want.

* * *

The division uses `2.0` rather than `2` so integer division doesn't truncate the result. With `2`, `(19 + 21) / 2` would give `20` as an `int` and lose the decimal.

* * *

`Arrays.sort` runs in O(n log n) time, which is fine for typical use. If you needed to handle huge arrays or streaming data, there are selection algorithms that find the median in O(n) time without a full sort.

* * *

One edge case worth deciding on: an empty array has no median. I throw an exception, but you might prefer returning `Double.NaN` or `OptionalDouble` depending on your context.

* * *

For very large values, watch out for overflow when averaging the two middle elements. Casting to `long` or `double` before adding avoids that.

