Your snippet is close, but it mixes two ideas. `AddHours(5)` is a fixed shift, not a time zone conversion — if you truly want "5 hours from local," it works, but it ignores daylight saving. If you want a real zone offset, use `DateTimeOffset` with a `TimeSpan`.

Multistep fix:
1. Grab the instant: `DateTime.Now`.
2. Build a `TimeSpan` of five hours.
3. Add it via `Add`, keeping the kind.
4. Format with `"yyyy-MM-dd hh:mm:ss tt"` — note `hh` is 12-hour, so `tt` gives AM/PM. If you want 24-hour, use `HH` and drop `tt`.
5. Print it.

```csharp
DateTime currentDateTime = DateTime.Now;
TimeSpan fiveHours = TimeSpan.FromHours(5);
DateTime offsetDateTime = currentDateTime.Add(fiveHours);
string formattedDateTime = offsetDateTime.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd hh:mm:ss tt");
Console.WriteLine(formattedDateTime);
```

This is identical in result to your version, just explicit. For genuine time zone work, `TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime` is the right tool.

here's a simple c# program that removes all occurrences of a given item from an array.

```csharp
using system;
using system.collections.generic;

class program
{
    static void main()
    {
        int[] input = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 2, 3, 4 };
        int itemtoremove = 2;

        list<int> result = new list<int>();
        foreach (int n in input)
        {
            if (n != itemtoremove)
                result.add(n);
        }

        console.writeline(string.join(", ", result));
    }
}
```

this prints `1, 3, 4, 5, 3, 4`.

if you want to keep it as an array, use `result.toarray()`. if you need to do it in place without a list, you can shift elements and resize, but the list version is clearer and fine for most cases.

Here's a straightforward implementation:

```csharp
public static string InsertChar(string input, char c, int index)
{
    if (input == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(input));
    if (index < 0 || index > input.Length)
        throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(index));

    return input.Insert(index, c.ToString());
}
```

`string.Insert` already does exactly this, so wrapping it is mostly about validating the index. If you want to avoid the intermediate string from `c.ToString()`, you can build it manually:

```csharp
public static string InsertChar(string input, char c, int index)
{
    if (input == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(input));
    if (index < 0 || index > input.Length)
        throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(index));

    return input.Substring(0, index) + c + input.Substring(index);
}
```

Both are O(n). For repeated insertions, a `StringBuilder` is faster.

******

Two versions, one using the built-in method and one manual:

```csharp
// Simple: relies on string.Insert
public static string InsertAt(string s, char c, int index)
{
    if (s == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(s));
    if (index < 0 || index > s.Length)
        throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(index));
    return s.Insert(index, c.ToString());
}
```

```csharp
// Manual: no intermediate string allocation from c.ToString()
public static string InsertAt(string s, char c, int index)
{
    if (s == null) throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(s));
    if (index < 0 || index > s.Length)
        throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(index));

    var chars = new char[s.Length + 1];
    s.CopyTo(0, chars, 0, index);
    chars[index] = c;
    s.CopyTo(index, chars, index + 1, s.Length - index);
    return new string(chars);
}
```

The first is clearer; the second avoids one allocation. Both throw on a bad index rather than silently clamping, which is usually what you want.

